Content-Type: text/plain;
	charset=utf-8

Since this specification can't both update and obsolete RFC 8422 and =
making the abstract and header match makes sense. But, I think the last =
paragraph of s1 also needs a sentence that explains how/why RFC 8422 is =
obsoleted. Will add this comment to the PR.

spt

> On May 6, 2026, at 12:56, Eric Rescorla <ekr@rtfm.com> wrote:
>=20
> Thanks for the PR. I'll take it you're in favor of this change. Anyone =
else want to weigh in?
>=20
> -Ekr
>=20
>=20
>=20
> On Wed, May 6, 2026 at 3:41=E2=80=AFAM John Mattsson =
<john.mattsson@ericsson.com <mailto:john.mattsson@ericsson.com>> wrote:
>> I tried to make an PR fixing the inconsistencies between abstract and =
header:
>> - Adding all obsolete drafts from the abstract to the heading
>> - fixing that 8422 is not both updated and obsoleted
>> - Changed "Negotiated Groups" to "Supported Groups". The term =
"Negotiated Groups"
>> is only used once and never again.
>>=20
>> =
https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/Raci4Lxm1Tk9IxrCpyQgJHMlXBw/
>>=20
>> Eric Rescorla wrote:
>> >I'm now trying to recall why we did this. ISTM that given that we =
are
>> >obsoleting 5246 (already done in 8446), we should obsolete all the
>> >other specs that only meaningfully apply to 5246. Here's the
>> >list:
>> >
>> > * RFC 5077: Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Resumption =
without
>> >Server-Side State
>> > * RFC 5246: The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.2
>> > * RFC 5705: Keying Material Exporters for Transport Layer Security =
(TLS)
>> > * RFC 6066: Transport Layer Security (TLS) Extensions: Extension
>> >Definitions
>> > * RFC 6961: The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Multiple Certificate =
Status
>> >Request Extension
>> > * RFC 7627: Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Hash and =
Extended
>> >Master Secret Extension
>> > * RFC 8422: Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Cipher Suites for =
Transport
>> >Layer Security (TLS)
>> >   Versions 1.2 and Earlier
>>=20
>> Note that 5705, 6066, and 7627 are listed as updated and not =
obsoleted
>>=20
>> Cheers,
>> John Preu=C3=9F Mattsson
>>=20
>>=20
>> From: Eric Rescorla <ekr@rtfm.com <mailto:ekr@rtfm.com>>
>> Date: Wednesday, 6 May 2026 at 01:30
>> To: John Mattsson <john.mattsson@ericsson.com =
<mailto:john.mattsson@ericsson.com>>
>> Cc: tls@ietf.org <mailto:tls@ietf.org> <tls@ietf.org =
<mailto:tls@ietf.org>>
>> Subject: Re: [TLS] Re: rfc8446bis status
>>=20
>>=20
>>=20
>> On Tue, May 5, 2026 at 2:21=E2=80=AFAM John Mattsson =
<john.mattsson@ericsson.com <mailto:john.mattsson@ericsson.com>> wrote:
>> Hi,
>>=20
>> I looked at  https://tlswg.org/tls13-spec/rfc9846.txt
>> and found some things that I think should be fixed in AUTH48.
>> I made a PR for the two easy editorial corrections =
https://github.com/tlswg/tls13-spec/pull/1416/changes
>>=20
>> Cheers,
>> John Preu=C3=9F Mattsson
>>=20
>> ----
>>=20
>> The heading and abstract are not aligned.
>> - The heading says it only obsoletes 8446, while the abstract says =
5077, 5246, 6961, 8422, and 8446
>> - The heading says 8422 is updates, while the abstract says =
obsoleted.
>>=20
>> "Obsoletes: 8446 (if approved)"
>> "Updates: 5705, 6066, 7627, 8422 (if approved)=E2=80=9D
>>=20
>> "This document updates RFCs 5705, 6066, 7627, and 8422 and obsoletes =
RFCs 5077, 5246, 6961, 8422, and 8446."
>>=20
>> I'm now trying to recall why we did this. ISTM that given that we are
>> obsoleting 5246 (already done in 8446), we should obsolete all the
>> other specs that only meaningfully apply to 5246. Here's the
>> list:
>>=20
>>  * RFC 5077: Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Resumption =
without Server-Side State
>>  * RFC 5246: The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol Version 1.2
>>  * RFC 5705: Keying Material Exporters for Transport Layer Security =
(TLS)
>>  * RFC 6066: Transport Layer Security (TLS) Extensions: Extension =
Definitions
>>  * RFC 6961: The Transport Layer Security (TLS) Multiple Certificate =
Status Request Extension
>>  * RFC 7627: Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Hash and Extended =
Master Secret Extension
>>  * RFC 8422: Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) Cipher Suites for =
Transport Layer Security (TLS)
>>    Versions 1.2 and Earlier
>>=20
>> ISTM that this standard applies to all of them, so we should just =
mark
>> them all Obsoletes.
>>=20
>>=20
>>=20
>> OLD: record_size_limit [RFC8849]=20
>> NEW: record_size_limit [RFC8449]
>>=20
>> Fixed in auth48 branch.
>> =20
>>=20
>> ---
>>=20
>> OLD: as described in Section 4.1.4).
>> NEW:  as described in Section 4.1.4.
>>=20
>> Fixed in auth48 branch.
>> =20
>>=20
>> ---
>>=20
>> "A client sending a ClientHello MUST support all parameters =
advertised in it"
>>=20
>> Shouldn't this be "MUST support all non-GREASE [RFC8701] parameters"=20=

>>=20
>> See:=20
>> https://github.com/tlswg/tls13-spec/pull/1421
>>=20
>> -Ekr
>> =20
>>=20
>> ---
>>=20
>>=20
>>=20
>>=20
>> From: Rob Sayre <sayrer@gmail.com <mailto:sayrer@gmail.com>>
>> Date: Friday, 20 March 2026 at 20:27
>> To: Eric Rescorla <ekr@rtfm.com <mailto:ekr@rtfm.com>>
>> Cc: TLS@ietf.org <mailto:TLS@ietf.org> <tls@ietf.org =
<mailto:tls@ietf.org>>
>> Subject: [TLS] Re: rfc8446bis status
>>=20
>> --=20
>>=20
>>=20
>>=20
>> On Fri, Mar 20, 2026 at 12:21=E2=80=AFPM Eric Rescorla <ekr@rtfm.com =
<mailto:ekr@rtfm.com>> wrote:
>> On Fri, Mar 20, 2026 at 12:19=E2=80=AFPM Rob Sayre <sayrer@gmail.com =
<mailto:sayrer@gmail.com>> wrote:
>> Hi,
>>=20
>> https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-rfc8446bis/history/
>>=20
>> has been in AUTH48 for 3 months now. What's the holdup?
>>=20
>> The holdup is that we're working through some last minute issues, =
such as https://github.com/tlswg/tls13-spec/pull/1410
>>=20
>> =20
>> I need to cite it.
>>=20
>> Cite 8446.=20
>>=20
>>=20
>> Oh I would, but I need to say the equivalent of "master secret".
>>=20
>> thanks,
>> Rob
